From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Meir Dick <mdick85@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 14:35:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: tas2783: do not block system resume on peripheral
 re-attach

tas2783_sdca_dev_resume() is installed for both RUNTIME_PM_OPS and
SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS. When the peripheral was detached it waits up to
TAS2783_PROBE_TIMEOUT (5s) for initialization_complete before restoring
the register cache.

The Master clears peripheral status over system suspend, so
slave->unattach_request is always set on system resume and the wait is
always taken. What it waits for is re-enumeration followed by
tas_io_init(), which issues a software reset and then a
request_firmware_nowait() whose completion it waits for. That work runs
in bus context and is serialized across the peripherals on the link, so
on a machine with four amplifiers it takes several seconds in total.

dpm_resume() runs with userspace frozen, so the whole system waits.
Measured on an HP EliteBook X G2i (four TAS2783 on link 2) with
/sys/power/pm_print_times enabled:

  slave-tas2783 sdw:0:2:0102:0000:01:9: acpi_subsys_resume returned -110 after 6805123 usecs
  slave-tas2783 sdw:0:2:0102:0000:01:a: acpi_subsys_resume returned 0 after 2358742 usecs
  slave-tas2783 sdw:0:2:0102:0000:01:c: acpi_subsys_resume returned 0 after 64217 usecs
  slave-tas2783 sdw:0:2:0102:0000:01:d: acpi_subsys_resume returned 0 after 48221 usecs

That is 9.2s of a 9.7s resume phase. Every other device on the machine,
including the GPU, resumes in under 400ms, and the display cannot come
back until the last of these returns.

The amplifier that reports the timeout is not faulty. Resume order is
the reverse of enumeration order, so the peripheral that attaches last
(device_number 4) is the first one waited on, and it absorbs the head of
a job that only finishes once all four have been re-initialized. All
four completions land within ~112ms of each other at the end. Reordering
the list would only move the timeout to whichever peripheral went first.

Blocking is reasonable for runtime resume, where only the audio path
waits. It is not reasonable for system resume. Give system sleep its own
callback that returns without waiting, leaving the regmap in cache-only
mode. tas_update_status() already restores the cache and calls
tas_io_init() when the peripheral re-attaches, so nothing is lost;
playback cannot start before the card has resumed in any case.

Clear slave->unattach_request there as well, since the system resume
path no longer does it. The runtime path still clears it after its own
wait.
---
 sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c | 48 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 46 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c
@@ -1103,8 +1103,40 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static s32 tas2783_sdca_dev_system_resume(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct sdw_slave *slave = dev_to_sdw_dev(dev);
+	struct tas2783_prv *tas_dev = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * If the peripheral stayed attached there is nothing to wait for, so
+	 * restore the cache here as the runtime resume path does.
+	 */
+	if (!slave->unattach_request) {
+		regcache_cache_only(tas_dev->regmap, false);
+		regcache_sync(tas_dev->regmap);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The peripheral was detached by the Master. Re-enumeration and the
+	 * firmware download that follows it run asynchronously in bus context,
+	 * and tas_update_status() restores the cache once the peripheral is
+	 * back. Blocking here would hold the system resume path - and with it
+	 * every frozen task - for as long as that takes.
+	 *
+	 * On a four amplifier machine the downloads are serialized on the bus
+	 * and take several seconds in total, which is long enough to be
+	 * visible to the user as a delay before the display comes back. Leave
+	 * the regmap in cache-only mode and return; playback cannot start
+	 * before the card resumes anyway.
+	 */
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static const struct dev_pm_ops tas2783_sdca_pm = {
-	SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(tas2783_sdca_dev_system_suspend, tas2783_sdca_dev_resume)
+	SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(tas2783_sdca_dev_system_suspend,
+			    tas2783_sdca_dev_system_resume)
 	RUNTIME_PM_OPS(tas2783_sdca_dev_suspend, tas2783_sdca_dev_resume, NULL)
 };
 
@@ -1193,6 +1225,7 @@
 {
 	struct tas2783_prv *tas_dev = dev_get_drvdata(&slave->dev);
 	struct device *dev = &slave->dev;
+	s32 ret;
 
 	dev_dbg(dev, "Peripheral status = %s",
 		status == SDW_SLAVE_UNATTACHED ? "unattached" :
@@ -1213,7 +1246,18 @@
 	regcache_sync(tas_dev->regmap);
 
 	/* perform I/O transfers required for Slave initialization */
-	return tas_io_init(&slave->dev, slave);
+	ret = tas_io_init(&slave->dev, slave);
+
+	/*
+	 * The system resume path returns without waiting for
+	 * initialization_complete, so clear the re-attach request here, once
+	 * the peripheral really has been re-initialized. The runtime resume
+	 * path still clears it after its own wait.
+	 */
+	if (!ret)
+		slave->unattach_request = 0;
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
